========================================================================
NOT COVERED BY EITHER LICENSE — third-party game data
========================================================================

The files listed below carry data this project did not author. To the extent
any of it is taken from the client of The Elder Scrolls: Blades, it is NOT our
copyright and is NOT licensed to you by us; all rights in it remain with its
owner (ZeniMax Media Inc. / Bethesda Softworks LLC). It is here for the sole
purpose of interoperating with that client, so that a game whose servers were
shut down can still be played and studied.

These files are not uniform in origin, and the distinction matters to anyone
redistributing them. Three kinds of content are mixed together:

  1. FROM THE CLIENT — read out of the shipped game data. This is the part
     the disclaimer above is about.
  2. FROM NETWORK OBSERVATION — recorded from the retail service while it
     was running. These are measurements of how a system behaved.
  3. AUTHORED BY THIS PROJECT — models built where retail's own values were
     never observable because they existed only on the server. The per-level
     shop generation in `shop_stock.json` is the clearest example; its own
     metadata marks that part "NOT AVAILABLE (server-only, uncaptured)".
     Category 3 is our work, is our copyright, and is covered by the AGPL
     like any other file here.

We have not tried to split the files along these lines, because a single file
routinely contains all three. Treat the whole set with the caution that
category 1 warrants.

  deploy/static/*.json                     33 files
  server/data/static/*.json                 5 files
  server/src/arena/combat/gamedata.rs      generated tuning tables

  Together ~5.3 MB: about 154,000 lines of JSON and 40,000 lines of
  generated Rust.

WHAT THESE FILES CONTAIN

Overwhelmingly identifiers and measurements, not authored content:

  * UUIDs naming items, dungeons, quests, spawn groups and shops;
  * numeric tuning tables — durability, sell prices, repair and upgrade
    costs, damage and resistance values, drop weights;
  * structural relationships — which spawn group belongs to which dungeon,
    which recipe yields which item, which shop stocks which bundle;
  * localisation KEYS such as "Items.Name.ChaurusArmor". These are lookup
    identifiers. The strings a player actually reads are NOT here: no
    localisation table has ever been committed to this repository.

One category is different, and we would rather name it than bury it:
`gamedata.rs` embeds roughly 850 item names — "Maul of the Aureal",
"Gauntlets of the Companions" and the like. Individually these are short
identifiers the wire protocol requires, but taken together they are a
curated set of the publisher's naming, and that is a stronger claim than
anything else in this list.

Prose appearing in these files under `_meta` keys is documentation written
by this project's contributors describing how the data was derived. That
text is ours and is covered by the AGPL, not by this section.

WHAT IS NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY

Stated because it materially narrows what you are handling:

  * no game assets of any kind — no textures, models, audio, video or fonts;
  * no client binaries, no APK or IPA, no decompiled client source;
  * no localised game text, dialogue, quest writing or item descriptions;
  * nothing that has ever been committed here in binary form.

The entire history of this repository has been checked for the above.

IF YOU REDISTRIBUTE THIS

You are responsible for your own assessment of these files; nothing here is
legal advice, and the characterisation above is our honest description of
the contents rather than a warranty about their status.

If you would prefer to distribute without the category 1 material, much of it
can be regenerated from a client installation you supply yourself, using the
extraction tooling this project publishes. Be aware that this is NOT true of
the whole set: the category 2 and 3 content cannot be regenerated from a
client at all — the retail service it was observed from no longer exists, and
the authored models were never in the client to begin with. Removing the
game-derived data therefore leaves a server that is missing values rather than
one that rebuilds them. See docs/LICENSING.md.
